The final project will be making a flipbook. This is meant to be a culmination of everything we have discussed and examined so far this term. The exact meaning of what a “flipbook” is is up to you to interpret, and you do not have to make a physical book if your concept/idea works better purely digitally. However, you MUST adhere to the following guidelines:

# If making a physical book, or using stop-motion, you must have at minimum 90 pages/frames. If you are doing something digital, and the piece has a concrete duration, it must last at least 3 seconds … For those that are doing something more interactive, or web or generative, these rules might not apply, so ask me for further clarification.
# You must be able to explain how your piece qualifies as a "book"
#* if you are making a physical flipbook then you semi-automatically accomplish this…
#* if doing something on screen, you might need to explain what you think "book" means in this new space.
# This needs to be a piece of graphic design still. How does time, motion, and graphic design translate into the space of a book? Analyze this in your design work.
# I'd like you all to keep thinking about the different things we've been talking about all term — physics, time, the universe expanding, light speed, entropy, systems … etc. These are all things that I find important when working with Time and Motion in Design, but also just for design in general. However, I think the Maholy-Nagy + Futurist essays will bring up some interesting ideas about how traditional 2D mediums can still embody the ideas of time and motion without having to be films or interactive works.

!! Prompts

Make a “flipbook” that covers uses these ideas/elements as prompts:

* A. Time + Motion - what does this mean to you?
* B. Narrative + Storytelling - part of the advantage to sequential art is that it can easily carry a narrative, I want you to come up with some sort of narrative device for your flipbooks.
* C. Entropy - this is the idea that everything in the universe tends towards chaos, no matter how ordered you try to make it.

!! Requirements

# Either Stop Motion or a Physical book (or both)
# @ the minimum 50 frames
# Discusses the ideas of Time + Motion, & Entropy
# Some sort of “narrative”

