! Earlier version: Fall 2010, Graphic Design 3, Project 3 (final project)

For our final project you will all be making a flipbook.

This is meant to be a culmination of everything we have discussed and examined so far this term. Now the exact meaning of what a “flipbook” is is up to you to interpret, and you do not have to make a physical book if your concept/idea works better purely digitally. However, you MUST adhere to one of these two guidelines:

# Use of Stop Motion - the idea of the flipbook is to actually capture things frame by frame, so my preference is that you actually make each frame.
# If you don’t use stop motion (IE use tweening to create your transitions) you MUST output your design-a-mation as a physical book. I repeat — if you do not make a stop motion design-a-mation, you MUST print out and make a physical flipbook of your work.

ALSO the bare minimum is 50 frames … if you have less than this, and you actually construct a physical book, there is no way you’ll have enough paper to actually grip and flip. I would say shoot for higher than that, as it will give you more opportunity for narrative—50 frames is the BARE MINIMUM.

!! Requirements

# Either Stop Motion or a Physical book or both
# @ the minimum 50 frames
# When making physical books you must make at least 2 flipbooks so that I can keep one if you make a physical version (an edition of 5 probably isn't a bad idea, being able to trade projects amongst each other is always valuable)
# Discusses the ideas of Time + Motion, & Entropy
# Must have some sort of “narrative”
# Make sure the book / animation feels “Designed” …

The rest is up to you.

!! Helpful Hints / Contstruction Guidelines

; Paper stock
: note cards work well, a little thinner than this would be fine too
; Size/Shape
: usually better if slightly wider than tall, remember, you only see about 1/3 of the outer edge of the page in a real, physical flipbook when flipping …
; conceptual
: why does this need to be in motion
: how does “flipping” through aid the concept
; needs “design” components … type?
: Remember to aim for “graphic design” over animation or illustration
; Don’t forget that once you get to the end of the flipbook you can turn it over and flipback
: this could be an interesting conceptual element to the piece—this is also a way to use less physical pages, but still achieve the same minimum 70 pages/frames/slides

